<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcus Aurelius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">121–<a href="/years/180">180</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcus Aurelius was Roman emperor from 161 to 180, ruling jointly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-verus">Lucius Verus</a> until 169. His reign was dominated by war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, the Antonine Plague and, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> against the Marcomanni, Quadi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>. His philosophical notebook, the Meditations, is a classic of Stoic thought.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius.jpg" alt="Marcus Aurelius" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Martin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MSR-ra-61-b-1-DM.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…route from Malaysia to central Vietnam. Óc Eo, its port and entrepôt, has yielded Roman, Persian, Indian and Greek artefacts: Louis Malleret found there in the 1940s gold medallions of Antoninus Pius and <b>Marcus Aurelius</b> and locally made gold discs imitating coins of Antoninus, Commodus and Septimius Severus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Iazyges, who settled between the Danube and the Tisza, destroyed a Roman legion in Domitian's Sarmatian war of 92 CE; the Roxolani joined Decebalus of Dacia against Trajan; and in the Marcomannic Wars the Iazyges fought <b>Marcus Aurelius</b> until their king Zanticus made peace in 175 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first important expansion came late in the reign of Antoninus Pius and under <b>Marcus Aurelius</b>, and the cult reached the height of its popularity in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, spreading at an “astonishing” rate.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Roman rule, Greek remained the language of the eastern half of the empire and of philosophy, medicine and science throughout it; educated Romans learned Greek, and the emperor <b>Marcus Aurelius</b> wrote his Meditations in it.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Hadrian made Antoninus his successor on condition that he adopt <b>Marcus Aurelius</b> and Lucius Verus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Twelve early Christians from the town of Scillium in North Africa were executed by the Roman Empire in Carthage, at the end of a wave of persecution under <b>Marcus Aurelius</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-17">17 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Vindobona, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=159" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Marcus Aurelius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=159">What was here in 159 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do not act as if thou wert going to live ten thousand years. Death hangs over thee. While thou livest, while it is in thy power, be good.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 4 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;No longer talk at all about the kind of man that a good man ought to be, but be such.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 10 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Begin the morning by saying to thyself, I shall meet with the busybody, the ungrateful, arrogant, deceitful, envious, unsocial.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 2 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The universe is transformation: life is opinion.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 4 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for the mind converts and changes every hindrance to its activity into an aid; and so that which is a hindrance is made a furtherance to an act; and that which is an obstacle on the road helps us on this road.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 5 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Alexander the Macedonian and his groom by death were brought to the same state; for either they were received among the same seminal principles of the universe, or they were alike dispersed among the atoms.&#8221;</p><footer>Meditations, book 6 &#183; tr. George Long, 1862 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15877/pg15877-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
